WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:13.870
After being assaulted, Fix finally stood up.

2
00:00:13.871 --> 00:00:18.540
He had an enormous bruise on his cheek, and his clothes were in tatters.

3
00:00:18.541 --> 00:00:25.160
Luckily, Aouda had escaped unharmed. Mr. Fogg was covered in dust, but he was fine.

4
00:00:25.161 --> 00:00:30.281
As soon as they were out of the crowd, Mr. Fogg thanked the detective.

5
00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:35.352
"No thanks are necessary," replied Fix. "But let us go!"

6
00:00:35.353 --> 00:00:37.556
"Where?" asked Aouda.

7
00:00:37.557 --> 00:00:42.566
"To a tailor!" He laughed, showing them his torn clothes.

8
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:47.764
An hour later they returned to the hotel with Fix more suitably dressed.

9
00:00:47.765 --> 00:00:53.744
Passepartout was there, waiting for his master and armed with half a dozen six-barreled revolvers.

10
00:00:53.745 --> 00:01:02.132
When Passepartout saw Fix, he frowned. His expression changed, however, when Aouda explained their encounter with Colonel Proctor.

11
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:06.483
"Fix seems to be keeping his word," Passepartout thought.

12
00:01:06.484 --> 00:01:10.721
At a quarter before six, the travelers reached the train station.

13
00:01:10.722 --> 00:01:18.610
It was already evening, cold and cheerless, and the sky was overcast. The train left the station on time.

14
00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:26.242
Even though it proceeded slowly, pausing at every stop along the line, the train kept Mr. Fogg and his companions on schedule.

15
00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:31.003
At eight o'clock, the steward entered the car and announced that it was time to go to bed.

16
00:01:31.004 --> 00:01:39.840
The backs of the seats were lowered, bedrolls were unpacked, and soon each traveler had a comfortable bed with clean sheets and a soft pillow.

17
00:01:39.841 --> 00:01:47.408
The next morning, after eating breakfast, Mr. Fogg and his companions watched the landscape of the vast prairies.

18
00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:52.525
Mountains lined the horizon, and creeks and rivers passed by their windows.

19
00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:58.487
Around noon the train approached a herd of ten—or twelve—thousand buffalo that were crossing the tracks.

20
00:01:58.488 --> 00:02:04.426
The train slowed, attempting to push through, but the mass of animals was too great.

21
00:02:04.427 --> 00:02:09.346
It took three full hours for the beasts to clear the tracks.

22
00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:14.928
By the time the last buffalo crossed the rails, the first had already reached the horizon.

23
00:02:14.929 --> 00:02:20.144
Passepartout was furious at the delay. "What a country!" he cried.

24
00:02:20.145 --> 00:02:26.002
"Mere cattle stop the trains. I would like to know if Mr. Fogg foresaw this delay!"

25
00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:31.835
While Passepartout was cursing impatiently, Aouda was growing anxious for a different reason.

26
00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:36.889
At the last stop, several passengers had walked up and down the platform for fresh air.

27
00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.425
Among them Aouda had recognized Colonel Proctor.

28
00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:45.505
Her heart pounded when she saw the man that Mr. Fogg had promised to challenge.

29
00:02:45.506 --> 00:02:50.661
It was necessary, at all costs, to prevent Mr. Fogg from seeing his adversary.

30
00:02:50.662 --> 00:02:55.813
Aouda seized a moment when Mr. Fogg was asleep to tell Fix and Passepartout.

31
00:02:55.814 --> 00:02:59.610
"Colonel Proctor on this train!" cried Fix.

32
00:02:59.611 --> 00:03:04.666
"Well, before he settles with Mr. Fogg, he has got to settle with me!"

33
00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:09.709
"Mr. Fix," replied Aouda, "Mr. Fogg will allow no one to avenge him.

34
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:17.105
He told me that he was prepared to return to America after his journey to find this man. He must not see Colonel Proctor!"

35
00:03:17.106 --> 00:03:21.035
Mr. Fogg woke up, and the conversation quickly stopped.

36
00:03:21.036 --> 00:03:24.537
Aouda madly searched for a way to distract Mr. Fogg.

37
00:03:24.538 --> 00:03:32.009
Suddenly an idea came to her: whist! She whispered her idea to Fix, and soon after a game began.

38
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:39.400
Fix knew the game well, and Aouda and Passepartout picked it up quickly. They played all morning.

39
00:03:39.401 --> 00:03:44.943
Shortly after lunch they had just begun a new round when the train let out a whistle and stopped.

40
00:03:44.944 --> 00:03:49.988
Passepartout put his head out the window but could see nothing that could have caused the delay.

41
00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.677
He jumped out of the car and found the signalman talking to the engineer.

42
00:03:54.678 --> 00:03:58.066
"No! You can't pass," the signalman was saying.

43
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:01.862
"The bridge at Medicine Creek is shaky and can't bear the weight of the train!

44
00:04:01.863 --> 00:04:04.940
The river is deep there. It's not safe."

45
00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:11.957
The passengers would have to walk along the river to a safe place to cross and then walk another six hours to the next station.

46
00:04:11.958 --> 00:04:16.926
Passepartout's heart almost stopped when he heard this news.

47
00:04:16.927 --> 00:04:22.853
By now a crowd had gathered around the engineer and the signalman, and an argument broke out.

48
00:04:22.854 --> 00:04:26.720
Finally the engineer calmed the crowd and suggested a daring plan.

49
00:04:26.721 --> 00:04:29.676
By accelerating the train to its very highest speed,

50
00:04:29.677 --> 00:04:36.092
they might have a chance of racing over the bridge before it collapsed into the rapids below.

51
00:04:36.093 --> 00:04:44.104
Passepartout was shocked. He was ready to try almost anything to get over Medicine Creek, but this solution was almost a little too daring!

52
00:04:44.105 --> 00:04:47.913
He ran back to the cabin to tell the others what was happening.

53
00:04:47.914 --> 00:04:51.802
"All aboard!" cried the conductor.

54
00:04:51.803 --> 00:04:58.212
The engineer reversed the train for almost a mile, and then, with a whistle, the train began moving forward again.

55
00:04:58.213 --> 00:05:04.398
The engine puffed huge clouds of steam as the train gained speed, going faster and faster.

56
00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:08.004
The cars shook and the windows rattled.

57
00:05:08.005 --> 00:05:12.197
It was like a frightful iron beast, racing to its destruction.

58
00:05:12.198 --> 00:05:16.466
Just before the river, the train exceeded a hundred miles an hour.

59
00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:20.096
It sailed over the bridge, hardly touching the rails at all!

60
00:05:20.097 --> 00:05:31.343
The last car had just reached the other side safely when the bridge fell with a crash into the rapids below.

61
00:05:31.344 --> 00:05:38.358
That was quite a train ride—being delayed by thousands of buffalo, speeding over a collapsing bridge . . . Whew!

62
00:05:38.359 --> 00:05:42.183
Aside from these incidents, our train journey has been quite enjoyable.

63
00:05:42.184 --> 00:05:48.024
We have been traveling over America's prairies, large areas of open land like fields or meadows.

64
00:05:48.025 --> 00:05:53.375
Tall grass and colorful wildflowers grow on the prairies, but there are very few trees.

65
00:05:53.376 --> 00:06:01.596
In rainy areas the grass grows very tall, so tall that you could ride a horse and no one would see you! Now that's tall!

66
00:06:01.597 --> 00:06:06.257
Many kinds of animals live here, including antelope, buffalo, and rabbits.

67
00:06:06.258 --> 00:06:11.116
Buffalo are the largest mammals in North America, standing close to six feet high.

68
00:06:11.117 --> 00:06:15.799
Even though they're big, they can run really fast, about 25 miles an hour.

69
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:25.484
So don't tease a buffalo because it'll catch you. And buffalo have horns. Ouch!
